Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Telegram Spat Teeth
- Story & Context: At the dry crossroads outpost of Bir Tarfawi, scrap-broker Mateo cracked a sun-bleached wooden parcel box addressed to a coastal radio station that had vanished twenty years earlier, uncovering twenty heat-warped Korg oscillator boards buried inside dried camel hide. The desert wind had blown caustic salt directly into the solder joints, but when Mateo struck his railway cowbell to announce the auction, a fugitive dancer grabbed a megaphone and began howling syncopated Cuban street verses over the howling, short-circuiting square waves. A drunken turf skirmish erupted instantly over the salvaged electronics, blood and oil splattering across the salt flat as the crowd locked into a terrifying, mud-thick timba stomp. Mateo grabbed the megaphone from the dirt and bellowed into the rising sandstorm: "The ink baked black before you called my name!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A cracked, yellowed telegram envelope half-swallowed by white desert sand, its edges charred and crumbling, while a heavy rusted iron cowbell lies adjacent with copper wires sprouting from its bell mouth into a swirling dust storm, cinematic hyper-detailed surrealism.

Track 2: Sister Amparo’s Lost Manifest
- Story & Context: Sheltered beneath an overturned tanker truck during a three-day sandstorm, Yocelyn huddled over a dying car battery to record an unsent confession letter to her estranged sister, listening to the relentless clank-clank-clank-ta-clank of Mateo's cowbell reverberating off the hollow steel hull. The air inside the cabin tasted of sulfur, scorched wire insulation, and damp wool as she drove a distorted baritone guitar riff through a melted delay pedal, matching the manic 3:2 clave of the storm outside. Between coughing fits caused by the fine alkali powder, she whispered and screamed into an intercom mic, confessing she had intentionally burned the transport convoy's navigation maps to stay marooned in the synth scrap yards. Trembling in the red glow of an overheating tube amp, she laid her cheek against the hot iron chassis and wept the track's hook: "I wrote your address on water, now watch the salt drink it dry."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An interior shot of an abandoned industrial truck cab encrusted with dry desert mud, lit by the eerie neon-red glow of glowing vacuum tubes, with an open postal envelope resting on a rusted dashboard covered in loose resistors and desert dust, poetic moody lighting.

Track 3: Tape Head Erasing the Salt
- Story & Context: Decades after Caravana Cero's solar bus ran out of water along the scorched perimeter of the Dakhla salt depression, scavengers discovered their reel-to-reel master machine fused into a solid block of sodium crystal and oxidized copper. When museum engineers in the capital gently heated the tape with halogen lamps, the warped magnetic ribbon unspooled a ghostly, polyrhythmic artifact where heavy, clattering timba bells and detuned sludge riffs bled together into an endless, psychedelic guajeo that sounded alive. The salt had eroded the tape’s oxide layer in jagged micro-patterns, creating accidental, impossible polyrhythms that seemed to mourn every traveler who died chasing salvaged signals across the waste. Etched into the aluminum take-up reel was one final scratched phrase: "No letter can cross the basin once the bells catch fire."
